data.content:
No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since you said "class library" with no DI, the **lightest** option depends on your .NET version:

---

### .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

Inject `TimeProvider` via the constructor — no custom interface needed:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Production:** pass `TimeProvider.System`.  
**Tests:** use `FakeTimeProvider` from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 8, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 9, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(2));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 9, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

---

### Pre-.NET 8 (or no DI at all) → Ambient `Clock` with `AsyncLocal`

If adding constructor parameters everywhere feels heavy, use an ambient context. It's safe for parallel tests thanks to `AsyncLocal<T>`:

```csharp
public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

Then replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` → `Clock.UtcNow` in your classes, and in tests:

```csharp
using (Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)))
{
    Assert.True(new InvoiceCalculator().IsOverdue(
        new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 9, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
}
```

---

**My recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — it's the framework-blessed answer and costs one constructor parameter per class. Want me to apply either approach to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
